WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:08.320
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Right, I think everyone, I hope&nbsp;
everyone is ready to start the public session&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:17.440
of the NHS England board meeting. And before&nbsp;
I ask, before I go into the main agenda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:25.600
and before Simon gives his report, I just wanted&nbsp;
to say, on behalf of the whole board, Simon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.600
and your whole team, that the way that the&nbsp;
NHS has coped with the extraordinary pressures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.360
over the last 10 months, the&nbsp;
way that it's contributed to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.080
scientific sort of the fight back against&nbsp;
the virus, and now the way in which it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:49.680
rolled out the vaccine programme has been heroic.
And I think when historians look back over the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.520
over the life of the NHS, you know, they&nbsp;
may well say that this may have been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:01.760
most difficult time but also that it was actually&nbsp;
their finest hour. And so, I just wanted to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.360
you know, how proud we are on the board to be, to&nbsp;
be part of this, and to thank you and your team.&nbsp;

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:13.120
And I know that David Behan and Andrew Morris,&nbsp;
who both have been in the health and social care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.840
world for all their working lives, would also&nbsp;
like to say something as well. So, David Behan.&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:27.680
&gt;&gt;David Behan: Yeah, thank you, David. And if I&nbsp;
could just add to your comments of appreciation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:33.360
and gratitude and admiration for the&nbsp;
way that NHS staff have operated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:41.120
And also, just pay tribute to the way that&nbsp;
services at a local level and at the national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:46.480
level have collaborated, you know, the list is&nbsp;
not just between acute, community and primary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:52.160
but social care staff, public health staff,&nbsp;
wider local government staff, the voluntary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:59.200
and community sector staff who are playing such&nbsp;
an important role in the vaccination programme.&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.920
But also, and they're not called out very often,&nbsp;
I'm very mindful of the contribution students are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:11.120
making to responding to the challenges of COVID&nbsp;
and the vaccine, and the way that universities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.520
who were key partners in the education of&nbsp;
students have also made that contribution.&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:21.600
So, I think what we've seen is real collaboration&nbsp;
across the sectors in responding to these three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:29.440
challenges of responding to COVID, continuing&nbsp;
to deliver services for those who require them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:35.520
and now delivering the vaccination programme.&nbsp;
And I think it's absolutely essential as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.360
look forward over the next 12 months,&nbsp;
we're beginning to take those lessons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:45.440
and build them into the way we move forward.
But I think this is just a moment to pay tribute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.640
to some quite outstanding collaborative leadership&nbsp;
and collaborative work which has been demonstrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.920
nationally and locally, David, thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, David. And Andrew.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.440
You're on mute, Andrew.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:08.000
Thanks, David. I've been involved in the NHS for&nbsp;
over 41 years. This has been an unprecedented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:14.240
response. It's brought the NHS together. It's,&nbsp;
it's ensured that, you know, we've done things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:21.200
very differently as part of our reaction to COVID.&nbsp;
I think we've seen some fantastic leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:27.200
right across all of our organisations,&nbsp;
but particularly from Simon and the team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.880
and it's truly been an outstanding contribution.
Without their input, I think the NHS would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.280
have been in a worse place. And more&nbsp;
importantly, we wouldn't have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.000
able to respond to patients in the way that we&nbsp;
have done. So really great stuff. Thank you.&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.040
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Thanks, Andrew.&nbsp;
Right, moving on to the main agenda, then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:54.000
we have one apology for absence. Ara Darzi’s&nbsp;
had to go off and do some clinical work at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.840
Imperial, at St Mary’s. Any declarations&nbsp;
of interest that aren't already declared?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:07.680
No. And I think we can approve the minutes of the&nbsp;
last board meeting on at the end of November. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.760
Simon, on to you for your report, please.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:19.200
Okay, well, thank you, David and colleagues.&nbsp;
And I think the first point to make is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:26.560
this Saturday marks the one-year anniversary&nbsp;
of the World Health Organization declaring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:33.680
a public health emergency of international concern&nbsp;
– the official beginning of the COVID pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.720
although we now know that actually it had&nbsp;
been brewing for some months before that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:45.600
And that same day, we put the NHS onto the&nbsp;
highest level of national incident emergency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.920
EPRR level four, and frankly, the intensity&nbsp;
and the relentlessness has continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:59.120
ever since. And that of course has had&nbsp;
a huge burden and pressure on services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.840
particularly on our staff but to be&nbsp;
truthful every family across the country.&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:12.400
And, frankly, matters have got worse since our&nbsp;
last public board meeting for NHS England and NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:19.840
Improvement. The position in November, December&nbsp;
and January is such that we've treated more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:26.560
severely ill coronavirus patients in hospital in&nbsp;
those three months than we did in the entire eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:35.680
months preceding that, March through October.
And today we've got just under 31,000 hospitalised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:43.040
coronavirus patients across the country. And it&nbsp;
is a tribute frankly to the whole of the NHS that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:49.760
not only those coronavirus patients but all the&nbsp;
other patients that we're here to look after have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:55.200
continued to get support through this period. But&nbsp;
let's not disguise the fact that there clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.080
have been disruptive consequences as a result.
I think the work that was done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:08.400
by the NHS during the course of summer and autumn&nbsp;
to prepare for these increased pressures has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:15.840
paid dividends. There were essentially five&nbsp;
aspects to that. Firstly, the investment that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:23.920
been made in infrastructure and equipment, so 159&nbsp;
emergency departments getting capital investment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:30.400
the procurement of additional critical&nbsp;
care equipment and ventilators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:37.360
the creation of the facilities in which we've&nbsp;
been able to add 50% more critical care beds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.880
through surge capacity than was available&nbsp;
to us at the start of the pandemic. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:48.400
infrastructure clearly has been important.
Secondly, staff. And I know we will talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:55.920
this in more detail, but not only the staff&nbsp;
currently working in the health service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:00.880
but those who have come back to help.&nbsp;
Student nurses, undergraduate doctors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:07.920
international recruits, actually, perhaps&nbsp;
paradoxically, we've had the strongest staffing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.000
growth, the strongest increase in numbers&nbsp;
of staff working in the health service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.520
over this past year than we've had&nbsp;
in a decade. We obviously want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.640
hold on to that and support and build from that,&nbsp;
as we look out to next year and the years to come.&nbsp;

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:26.640
But as well as infrastructure and staff, of&nbsp;
course partnerships have been a huge part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:30.960
the preparation that has been undertaken.&nbsp;
The work that we've been doing with hospices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.240
with local authorities, with the independent&nbsp;
sector, with the voluntary sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:39.280
all of that has been hugely important.
Fourthly, the new treatments and care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:45.120
that has been developed, whether that's online&nbsp;
access, pulse oximetry, long COVID clinics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:51.760
the continuing evolution of critical care&nbsp;
treatments, particularly oxygen rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:56.800
ventilated support for some patients, and&nbsp;
improved clock speed of randomised control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:02.480
trials to develop new therapies, all of that&nbsp;
has been part of how the service has prepared.&nbsp;

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:09.920
And then fifthly I think during summer and&nbsp;
autumn, hospitals and primary care used that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.560
window of opportunity well to begin to tackle&nbsp;
some of the backlogs of care that had arisen.&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.760
But having said all of that, and that obviously&nbsp;
is at risk when coronavirus is out of control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:26.480
And so, as well as having 12,000 more COVID&nbsp;
inpatients now than we had in the April peak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:33.200
we're also looking after today 32,000&nbsp;
more hospital inpatients for conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:40.880
other than coronavirus, that double pressure.
So, although it does now look as if we are perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:48.320
at the top of the current wave, the chief medical&nbsp;
officer suggested we were probably at the peak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:53.840
now, we're just beginning to see inpatient numbers&nbsp;
beginning to turn. It looks as if it will be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:59.280
slow reduction in the number of inpatients that&nbsp;
we're dealing with, together with all of the needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:03.120
that we've discussed previously in the community&nbsp;
health services, mental health and elsewhere.&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:07.840
And at the same time, the vaccination&nbsp;
programmes are really building momentum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:14.160
one in seven adults now across England&nbsp;
having had their first vaccination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:20.800
And as we continue to make progress, that will&nbsp;
of course reduce the infection rate and indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:29.920
therefore reduce the pressures on the NHS.&nbsp;
About three quarters of general and acute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:37.520
hospital beds are occupied by people over the&nbsp;
age of 65 with COVID and about half of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:44.480
critical care beds. So, the connection between&nbsp;
vaccination and infection and reducing risk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:50.880
death and pressure on the NHS, that is obviously&nbsp;
what the next three and six months are going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.280
all about. We've got a specific item on&nbsp;
that so I will, I think, stop there, David.&nbsp;

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:03.520
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much. Does&nbsp;
anyone have any questions for Simon? I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:07.920
maybe I could just pick up one aspect of what&nbsp;
you're talking about, Simon, about the impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:14.160
that COVID’s had on our staff. And maybe you'd&nbsp;
like to bring in Prerana and Ruth and Stephen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.880
to that as well. But just maybe you just give your&nbsp;
impressions of what it's been like for the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:26.080
at the front line of coping with this crisis.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, in a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:35.280
because we've been so open and, you know, TV&nbsp;
news crews have been in hospitals and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:40.400
clinics up and down the country across the health&nbsp;
service, including over the last several weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:45.920
people can see that themselves. They can hear&nbsp;
people describe very directly what it means to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:52.240
be providing care under these circumstances. Long&nbsp;
shifts, fully togged in PPE, often dealing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:57.840
very distressed relatives as well as incredibly&nbsp;
sick patients. So, it's not just the intensity;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:04.000
it's the relentlessness and the duration&nbsp;
that I think has been taking its toll.&nbsp;

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:11.360
So, there are a series of things that local trusts&nbsp;
and employers with our support have been seeking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:20.880
to do in terms of health and wellbeing support,&nbsp;
psychological support. But frankly, what people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:27.840
I think first and foremost want in the here and&nbsp;
now is for the number of coronavirus patients that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:33.040
people are looking after to come down. And that&nbsp;
means getting infections coming down as well. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:36.960
then there are some big questions we're going to&nbsp;
have to answer about how to support staff coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:41.840
out the other side of this as we look to what&nbsp;
the future of health service will require.&nbsp;

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:47.360
Prerana and colleagues have begun that&nbsp;
dialogue with many of our partners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:52.080
so Prerana may want to come in now.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Prerana, would you like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.720
to add anything to what Simon has just said?
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Very briefly. Thank you, David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:03.680
and thank you, Simon. During the coronavirus, our&nbsp;
focus for the workforce response has been on staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:08.640
safety first and foremost. And in this context,&nbsp;
I first of all want to say thank you to everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:15.760
who is responding to the pandemic. But it's really&nbsp;
important that staff take up the vaccination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:21.920
COVID vaccination offer when it comes to them.&nbsp;
And that's currently our biggest focus for staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.760
safety. So please do take that up.
If you have any questions, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:32.560
lots of sources of information, especially&nbsp;
through the HR teams in your organisations.&nbsp;

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:36.800
The second is staff support. And again, the&nbsp;
health and wellbeing offer has been accessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:43.040
more than 620,000 times in the last 10&nbsp;
months. That's great, but we know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:48.160
there's more need so please do have a conversation&nbsp;
with your teams and line managers about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:54.480
support you need. And I think that's the message I&nbsp;
wanted to give to our colleagues in the frontline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:58.240
especially, is about accessing the support&nbsp;
that's there and not feeling guilty about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:03.040
Research has shown that health care professionals&nbsp;
are the last to seek support because of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:09.280
they are in the business of caring for everybody&nbsp;
else. So, our focus has been to shift that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:14.960
you know and make it easy to access the&nbsp;
help but also continually remind people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:20.000
that the support is there when they need it.
So, staff support, staff safety and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:26.080
workforce supply and getting more people&nbsp;
into the NHS because of course we know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:31.360
as Simon has described, that the workload is,&nbsp;
you know, is very, very high at this moment.&nbsp;

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:36.720
So, workforce supply, there's a lot of interest&nbsp;
in people joining the NHS, which is fantastic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:41.680
and we want to make sure that we capitalise&nbsp;
on that not only in the immediate term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:45.920
but in the medium to long term as well.
And then just one word on recovery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:52.720
which is it will be really important for all&nbsp;
of us to process the last year. And you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:57.920
I think most important for our NHS people. So,&nbsp;
we're working on the next phase of health and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:02.800
wellbeing support, which will focus on how&nbsp;
individuals and teams and organisations can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:12.640
process the last year but also get help for our&nbsp;
colleagues who need it. Some specialised support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:18.560
some support for teams, and then the very&nbsp;
important, you know, the importance of taking some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:25.440
rest and some leave. So, options to be as flexible&nbsp;
for employers as possible on annual leave and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:29.520
ability to plan around people taking some rest.
So, we are working with our clinical reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:35.280
group, which also includes leaders from the&nbsp;
military, from the WHO, who have experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:40.480
in multiple emergencies around the world, and&nbsp;
they are advising us on the recovery plans for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:47.360
our NHS people which of course need to be balanced&nbsp;
and you know, must take into account the services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:50.880
that need to come back as well. Thank you, David.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Prerana. Ruth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:57.520
did you want to add anything to that?
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you, David. So, it is relentless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:04.640
out there. And staff are working extraordinarily&nbsp;
hard. And when I've be working my part shifts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:08.640
at my local hospital when I've been&nbsp;
visiting Norfolk and Norwich this week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:16.000
it is truly tough. But it's also amazing just&nbsp;
how flexible these teams are being. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:22.960
not just one profession coming and working; we're&nbsp;
seeing teams of multiple professional backgrounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:29.440
working really well to step up to support&nbsp;
their patients and their relatives.&nbsp;

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:33.840
Whilst it's relentless, and&nbsp;
Prerana is absolutely right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:37.520
we need to carry on the focus of&nbsp;
supporting staff and continue to do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:46.560
I'm really grateful for those nurses from across&nbsp;
the globe, 6,000 nurses from across the globe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:52.800
until the end of November have come to&nbsp;
support us and become part of our work teams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.800 --> 00:16:00.960
And we've seen over 1,000 a month come and&nbsp;
support our NHS and be part of our longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:07.840
NHS. Our third-year students have come as well to&nbsp;
support us, and I'm grateful for colleagues at HEE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:13.680
and our university colleagues, and particularly&nbsp;
those third-year students that are willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:19.920
to step forward and be part of our teams.
And finally, I guess, I really want to say thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:28.160
you to those overseas nurses who have joined us,&nbsp;
that have joined the temporary register. Over 952&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:34.640
have joined our temporary registers since the 6th&nbsp;
of January this year. And together, they're making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:41.360
a real difference to the frontline teams, whether&nbsp;
that's in a mental health setting, critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:46.320
care setting, wherever they are. I personally&nbsp;
want to say a very big thank you to them all.&nbsp;

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:50.320
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Ruth. And Steve,&nbsp;
did you have a comment you'd like to make?&nbsp;

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:55.440
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Well, I think Simon,&nbsp;
Prerana and Ruth have really said it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.440 --> 00:17:01.680
but I would add of course, my thanks to all NHS&nbsp;
staff for everything they've done, and of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:06.720
all the volunteers and workers in other sectors.&nbsp;
The teachers and schools that have been looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:12.320
after the children of NHS staff where they've&nbsp;
been at work. So, I think NHS staff are really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:17.440
grateful for all the support they've had.
It's been a really relentless year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:22.640
as you've heard, and there are many tough months&nbsp;
unfortunately still to come in this pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:29.120
I've been struck with how well everybody&nbsp;
managed in the first wave in April and May,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:33.600
but then went into the waves we've seen&nbsp;
this winter with trepidation, but still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:39.440
have risen to the task, and have still adapted and&nbsp;
worked flexibly in ways that Ruth has described.&nbsp;

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:44.800
I think the final point I'd make is that&nbsp;
I think maybe Simon said at the start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:50.080
we are still treating and seeing many more&nbsp;
patients for conditions other than COVID,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:56.000
as we are with patients with COVID. And I think&nbsp;
staff are all so anxious and concerned to ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:01.360
that all patients get the treatment they need. And&nbsp;
as we've seen disruption because of the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:09.200
then there is anxiety too amongst staff for&nbsp;
those patients who we need to get in and treat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:15.120
as quickly as possible. And it's only really&nbsp;
by getting in control of the pandemic through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:20.320
the measures that are in place, but also the&nbsp;
vaccination programme, that we'll be able to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:26.320
back quickly to managing all patients in exactly&nbsp;
the way clinicians want to manage patients.&nbsp;

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:30.640
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Steve.&nbsp;
Simon, did you want to say anything else?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:36.720
Or should we move on to Amanda and Pauline?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: No, let's move on, I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:41.840
pass that on to Amanda.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Amanda.&nbsp;

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:50.480
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you very much indeed.&nbsp;
So, I think really just building on Simon's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:55.280
helpful introduction, we've got a couple of papers&nbsp;
here picking up a bit more on the particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.280 --> 00:19:02.400
operational performance of the NHS. And I think&nbsp;
the really important bit here is the context.&nbsp;

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:08.640
So, what we are describing is a combination&nbsp;
of exactly what Simon said, which is the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:15.200
that the NHS has responded to this really&nbsp;
significant pressure from COVID, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:21.360
the way that the NHS has continued to provide&nbsp;
for non COVID care across in particular these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:26.560
last few months of intense pressure.
And I think worth saying, Simon's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:31.280
run through some of the actions that were&nbsp;
taken obviously in advance of hitting this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:36.000
particular wave of pressure over the last&nbsp;
couple of months, just worth mentioning some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:41.920
of the actions that we have been taking over the&nbsp;
particular peak of pressure to make sure that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:47.040
NHS could still function as well as possible&nbsp;
given all of those different demands on it.&nbsp;

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:53.360
So Prerana, Ruth, Steve, have taught very&nbsp;
eloquently about all of the things that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:58.320
been going on around support for staff but also&nbsp;
making sure that we have the supply of staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:05.440
available to support the NHS through this, but&nbsp;
worth saying I think that some of the other things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:09.440
that we've done have really been about building&nbsp;
again on the mutual aid arrangements that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:13.840
in place for the first wave, particularly in&nbsp;
relation to critical care, which is where some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:17.760
of the greatest pressure has been seen.
So, we've seen organisations working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:25.120
together really effectively at a local level&nbsp;
within their regions and indeed nationally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:29.600
and in a very, very small number of cases,&nbsp;
that has been a very important part of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:34.160
response to just make sure that patients had&nbsp;
the care that they needed when they needed it.&nbsp;

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:38.000
The other thing that I think is just worth&nbsp;
mentioning is the work that's gone in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:44.240
really to supporting safe discharge. So we've had&nbsp;
a combination again there of some of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:49.120
that started in the first wave, so the discharge&nbsp;
to assess arrangements that were put in place then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:54.320
continuing through the year, but also, and again,&nbsp;
colleagues may want to say more about this, some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:59.840
of the new schemes, particularly around virtual&nbsp;
wards, providing both step up and step down care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:05.840
and those package of measures around discharge&nbsp;
have really played a very important role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:09.120
in I think helping to get through these&nbsp;
last couple of months. And that's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:13.520
absolutely where the partnership with local&nbsp;
governments, social care, voluntary sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:17.920
has also played an incredibly important role.
If I may just say a word, then just on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:22.800
maintenance of non COVID work. So, we have&nbsp;
seen if we look back to the first wave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:28.100
a much higher level of continued demand for&nbsp;
urgent emergency care. The work that Pauline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:32.720
has been leading around the whole redesign&nbsp;
and transformation of urgent emergency care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:38.960
once again, critical. So, the increase in demand&nbsp;
for 111 services is very much part of the planned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:44.800
response so that we were as resilient as possible&nbsp;
in giving people options to get access to care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:49.440
when they needed it, which didn't necessarily mean&nbsp;
going to an A&amp;E department, although of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:54.080
more people have continued to go to A&amp;E&nbsp;
departments across the last couple of months.&nbsp;

00:21:54.080 --> 00:22:00.800
Again, Pauline might want to talk about this,&nbsp;
but the publication of the, what do we call it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:06.640
it was the proposals that I think were, I’m&nbsp;
just checking the date, 15th of December,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:10.400
the transformation of urgent emergency&nbsp;
care, models of care and measurement report.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:15.360
Part again of that story about where we're going&nbsp;
in the future to continue to have really resilient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:22.240
but modernised urgent emergency care services.
Other things we've seen, we've seen a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:26.800
maintenance of the referrals, particularly&nbsp;
around cancer care. And that's one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:31.040
things we worried about a lot earlier in the&nbsp;
year, that patients weren't coming forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:36.160
So, I think that's a real good news story that&nbsp;
people have felt able to continue to come forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:44.000
through primary care to specialist diagnostic and&nbsp;
treatment services. We did again write out just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:50.800
before Christmas, to make sure that we published&nbsp;
clear advice to the NHS on the prioritisation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.800 --> 00:22:55.840
and maintenance of cancer services. And we've&nbsp;
obviously also just published the recovery plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.840 --> 00:23:00.720
around cancer services, which was developed with&nbsp;
a great deal of very helpful stakeholder input.&nbsp;

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:08.240
So, lots of activity now to make sure that we&nbsp;
really are maintaining. And good news story again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:11.360
on the maintenance of day&nbsp;
case activity, on diagnostics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:15.840
but lots of work going on in partnership with&nbsp;
the private sector, the independent sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.840 --> 00:23:22.800
to make sure that we are prioritising access&nbsp;
to urgent surgery, including cancer surgery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:26.640
while the NHS is under this particular&nbsp;
period of pressure at the moment.&nbsp;

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:32.160
The final thing I just wanted to draw attention to&nbsp;
if I may is mental health services. Mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:37.680
services have obviously maintained and remained&nbsp;
open throughout the whole of the past year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:45.360
and have focused on again, finding different&nbsp;
ways of being able to allow people to access them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:50.880
despite some of the pressures of the pandemic.
But we have seen a significant increase in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:55.040
demand for mental health services after the&nbsp;
first wave, we're expecting to see that again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.600 --> 00:24:01.280
after this wave, and one of the things&nbsp;
therefore that we're absolutely focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.280 --> 00:24:06.480
is continuing to expand services as per our&nbsp;
pre-existing long term plan commitments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.480 --> 00:24:11.760
So that just remains a really kind of core part&nbsp;
of the NHS’s response, both now and as we look to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:16.240
the future. If I stop there, David, is that okay,&nbsp;
I'm sure you’ll want to bring in other colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:20.800
to talk about any of those things and others.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks. Well, firstly, Pauline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:24.480
is there any aspects of Amanda's&nbsp;
report that you'd like to sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:29.280
go into a little bit more detail?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: So, David, I think most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:35.200
of the paper has been covered, but maybe&nbsp;
just to highlight one or two things. I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:42.400
first of all, you know, when we reflect back on&nbsp;
this period, I think, an enormous thanks to all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.400 --> 00:24:48.720
of our staff who are involved, and to all of the&nbsp;
people who are supporting our staff in the NHS.&nbsp;

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:54.080
But I think reflecting on what's been&nbsp;
happening over the last few months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.080 --> 00:25:01.440
I see two very strong things happening. The&nbsp;
first being the ongoing use of transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:08.080
that even in the toughest of times, our&nbsp;
staff have looked at how they can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:14.560
things differently, how they can innovate,&nbsp;
and how they can implement those initiatives.&nbsp;

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:20.640
So, for example in the paper we've set out 111&nbsp;
First, and you know, we've now got to a point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:27.840
where 28% of patients who contact 111 and who go&nbsp;
to emergency departments have booked appointments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:35.600
The COVID response service, the delivery of the&nbsp;
capital schemes that Simon referred to earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:40.960
the See and Treat, Hear and Treat pilots,&nbsp;
the adapt and adopt work across elective.&nbsp;

00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:48.000
But I think in addition to transformation, I think&nbsp;
my other memory from this period will definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:54.560
be the spirit of collaboration, mutual aid and&nbsp;
support that has existed throughout the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.560 --> 00:26:01.680
that we really have been one NHS. And that's right&nbsp;
across all of our operational areas of delivery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:06.640
whether it's our ambulance services,&nbsp;
critical care, elective care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:13.360
we've worked together across systems, across&nbsp;
regions, and basically with a lot of support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:20.640
also from some national colleagues who've been&nbsp;
involved in the overall work around critical care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:24.880
and some of our specialist commissioning&nbsp;
services. So, I think we've really worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:30.400
together as a single family during this period.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Pauline. And Ian, sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:34.960
picking up the theme of the transformation work&nbsp;
that has been going on throughout this period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.960 --> 00:26:39.440
it will be interesting for the board to&nbsp;
have your thoughts on what you've done to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.440 --> 00:26:43.920
treat more people outside hospital settings in&nbsp;
particular and improve discharge arrangements.&nbsp;

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:51.280
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Amanda highlighted that there has&nbsp;
been a really big focus on this over the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.280 --> 00:26:58.800
month, which has been I think more successful than&nbsp;
in many previous years. And I think a combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:05.280
of different things, David and colleagues,&nbsp;
including a number of new initiatives. COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:14.320
virtual wards, the use of independent sector,&nbsp;
use of hospices, hotels. I think the real trick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:22.800
as Pauline was saying, is how then we can maintain&nbsp;
that as we begin to come out of this current wave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:29.920
and that will be a major focus of my team.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Give an idea, Ian, of how many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:33.680
in terms of numbers of patients, that would&nbsp;
otherwise have been treated in hospital who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:38.480
are now being treated outside hospital.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So, if you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:45.600
the analysis we've done of the bed dividend&nbsp;
from our discharge arrangements, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:54.720
the discharge funding we have, it’s that it's a&nbsp;
sustained benefit of at least 5,000 beds. And when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.720 --> 00:28:00.560
you translate that into staff as well, that's more&nbsp;
than 10,000 staff benefit. And that's something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:06.480
obviously we are desperately keen to hang on to.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ian. I am going to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:06.480 --> 00:28:11.840
Wol, Wol, you have been encouraging the&nbsp;
board all along to be focusing on the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:16.160
Have you got any comment you'd like to make?
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Well, I think it's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.720 --> 00:28:23.280
it's really difficult in the midst of dealing&nbsp;
with a huge crisis, and let's not forget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:28.800
this isn't. this is the sort of, as Simon&nbsp;
and others have said, this is massively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:35.360
more impactful on staff than the first wave.
But we’ve got to look at the future. We’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:40.320
got to think hard about what we can do to&nbsp;
bake in some of the initiatives that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:45.360
paid dividends. And Ian just mentioned one,&nbsp;
which is absolutely critical, you know, the bed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:49.680
dividend. There have been some really interesting&nbsp;
and innovative methodologies which have applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.560 --> 00:28:56.880
that need to be focused on so that actually when&nbsp;
we come to winter 2021, we have that in the bank.&nbsp;

00:28:58.240 --> 00:29:02.400
Prerana talked about staff and workforce&nbsp;
and that is, without that workforce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.400 --> 00:29:09.120
we can't do anything. And you know, they're tired,&nbsp;
we've drained the social capital over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.120 --> 00:29:14.240
nine months, and we need to refill that. And you&nbsp;
know, from some of the work in the private sector&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:19.040
where this has happened, this is not quite as&nbsp;
acute obviously as the frontline NHS staff, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:24.640
we're seeing is that you just have to lean into&nbsp;
it and accept the fact that staff are stressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:30.400
and you've got to allow them to, in a sense, vent&nbsp;
and express how they feel about what's been going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:33.840
on before you can move to the next stage.
I know Prerana, and we're looking forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:39.680
as a board to seeing your report on the&nbsp;
initiative you're going to put in place.&nbsp;

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:43.840
And the final point I make is, and I think&nbsp;
others have mentioned this about inequalities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:49.360
And this is something that's critically important.&nbsp;
We need to again as a health system and as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:53.040
country face up to the inequalities of how&nbsp;
healthcare has been delivered in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:59.600
and how different groups access health care. And&nbsp;
this crisis has shone an extreme light on this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:04.160
and we need to make sure we don't leave that&nbsp;
behind. But I know that Simon in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:08.560
and Amanda, have been leaning into this very&nbsp;
strongly. And, you know, power to their elbow.&nbsp;

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:13.600
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Wol. So Ian, on&nbsp;
this point about health inequalities, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:22.160
give an update to the board of where we are.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So, in the summer, we published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:28.640
a report of the actions the NHS should&nbsp;
take straightaway to try and improve its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:34.960
performance on inequalities. Eight actions came&nbsp;
out in July, but since then, there's been I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:41.600
strong progress on most of those actions,&nbsp;
whether it's on reviewing digital inclusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:50.080
the continued work on health checks, and in&nbsp;
particular, a big focus on data and how do we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:58.400
embed analysis of performance, not just to the&nbsp;
whole population, but by the most deprived 20%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:03.280
of the population and different&nbsp;
specific BAME populations. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:07.440
I think that work is going really well.
Clearly, the main focus we have at the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:14.960
is on the COVID vaccination programme, and a&nbsp;
huge focus and effort by Emily and her team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.960 --> 00:31:20.080
working closely with local government&nbsp;
colleagues, in trying to make sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:27.600
we can maximise uptake in the most inclusive way.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ian. I'm looking at Andrew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:32.720
Morris. I mean, Andrew, given your long&nbsp;
experience of running hospitals in the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:39.280
you know, obviously Frimley being one of our&nbsp;
outstanding hospitals. I mean, how, in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:45.280
of the day-to-day operational pressures within a&nbsp;
hospital setting, just give a view to the board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.280 --> 00:31:50.480
of what it's like to be running a hospital today.
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: Thanks, David. So, I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:58.240
really tough. And every day requires, you know, a&nbsp;
different set of responses to the challenges that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:07.040
is faced by a particular hospital in a particular&nbsp;
patch. Flexibility around deployment of staff is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:13.200
really important, trying to get people&nbsp;
to, you know, cover all of the shifts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:21.120
on a 24-hour basis is a particular challenge.&nbsp;
And trying to get people to work very flexibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:28.240
is a challenge that all chief execs face.
So, we've seen anaesthetists who don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:34.400
normally work in a critical care setting, they've&nbsp;
stepped up to the plate and covered you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.400 --> 00:32:40.800
more beds than they would normally do in&nbsp;
their kind of day to day working life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:44.480
General physicians doing a lot&nbsp;
more than we've historically seen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:49.360
So, I think flexibility has been, you&nbsp;
know, the really, really key thing that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.360 --> 00:32:56.240
enabled us to respond in a really positive way.
And lots of staff have gone the extra mile. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.440 --> 00:33:04.880
now feel more as a single NHS than we did prior to&nbsp;
the pandemic. Because whether you're in a hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:11.120
setting or in primary care, or community service,&nbsp;
or in mental health, everyone is doing their bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:17.680
to help cope with this particular challenge.&nbsp;
And I think that's, that's given us a new glue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:26.000
to kind of lift ourselves up and look into the&nbsp;
future and create a different set of services that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:31.840
we offer patients. And, you know, there's some&nbsp;
real great transformational opportunities here.&nbsp;

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:38.160
But everyone, everyone has gone the extra&nbsp;
mile. And if we'd have sat here a year ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:38.800 --> 00:33:44.880
thinking that today we'd be talking about over&nbsp;
30,000 people with COVID in our hospital, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:50.240
would have said that's impossible, we would never&nbsp;
cope with it. And here we are, we're doing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:56.480
we're doing it really well under very difficult&nbsp;
circumstances, our staff are going the extra mile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.480 --> 00:34:04.640
day in day out and putting their families second&nbsp;
in many cases, to help, you know, the cause here.&nbsp;

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:10.400
So, you know, it is really, really one big team&nbsp;
effort. And that's the big thing that's come out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:10.400 --> 00:34:16.880
of this. So, it's a real challenge, but I think&nbsp;
it's controllable. Our big task ahead of us is to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.880 --> 00:34:23.200
you know, bring on as many elective services&nbsp;
as we can, give people a chance to, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:23.200 --> 00:34:30.320
recover from all of this so that they take time&nbsp;
out, have a bit of a break, but we still need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:30.320 --> 00:34:36.640
carry on doing the cancer work, the priority&nbsp;
one, two and three activity that we've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:36.640 --> 00:34:43.600
as a kind of elective agenda going forward. So,&nbsp;
it's being managed really, really well I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:49.040
but there are still some challenges ahead.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And Andrew and actually Amanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:52.480
wearing your old hat as chief executive of&nbsp;
the Guy’s and St Thomas’s, you were very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:57.040
much involved with integrated care in southeast&nbsp;
London, and I know for Frimley as well Andrew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:57.040 --> 00:35:01.040
you were very much at the forefront of&nbsp;
setting up an integrated care system around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:01.040 --> 00:35:05.440
Frimley. Do you think that the experience&nbsp;
of the last nine months has made the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:09.200
even stronger for integrated care?
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.200 --> 00:35:15.040
Most definitely from my perspective. So, some of&nbsp;
the things around getting people out of hospital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:21.360
avoiding people from coming into hospital in the&nbsp;
first place, you know, have really come to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:29.600
forefront. And this is the focus of the new ICSs,&nbsp;
and I'm sure they'll offer, you know, a higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.600 --> 00:35:35.600
quality service to our patients. Not everyone&nbsp;
needs to come into hospital. And every day as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:40.480
chief exec, there’ll be a proportion of patients,&nbsp;
a very significant proportion of patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.480 --> 00:35:44.080
where you scratch your head and think, well,&nbsp;
why are they here? They don't need to be here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:49.840
They don't need all of the technology that an&nbsp;
acute hospital offers; they're better off at home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:49.840 --> 00:35:55.360
being supported there or in another setting. And&nbsp;
that's the thing that's come to the forefront here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.360 --> 00:35:59.120
around different ways of working.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And Amanda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:59.840 --> 00:36:05.120
do you agree with that, largely?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: I 100% agree with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.120 --> 00:36:08.960
and I think it's something we've heard really&nbsp;
clearly, just to build on other people's comments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:09.680 --> 00:36:15.360
in terms of going forward out of the situation&nbsp;
we're in now, really building on that sense of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:21.680
NHS and partners as a single team, because that is&nbsp;
the reality of how it's been over the last year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:21.680 --> 00:36:26.160
And that started, you know, way back a&nbsp;
year ago with some of the mutual aid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.160 --> 00:36:31.760
building on some of the relationships that already&nbsp;
existed, and it has deepened and absolutely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.320 --> 00:36:35.680
you know, fast tracked the development of&nbsp;
some of those relationships and ways of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:39.760
working over the past year, and we absolutely&nbsp;
have heard very clearly people don't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.760 --> 00:36:43.520
go back, they want to go forward with that.
And I think the one thing I would just add to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:48.800
Andrew’s sort of very practical examples of where&nbsp;
that matters, is as we think about right now how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.800 --> 00:36:54.000
to maintain those, call them elective services,&nbsp;
but let's be clear, you know, these are people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:58.720
whose needs are real: they're not optional. And&nbsp;
particularly when we're talking about some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:04.480
our people who are in that more urgent category.
Actually, we've got some great examples, again, of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:04.480 --> 00:37:10.160
where coming together with systems to think about&nbsp;
how you use, for example, the independent sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.160 --> 00:37:15.200
or use some of, for example, again, the specialist&nbsp;
skills that exist in some places and not others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:15.200 --> 00:37:19.200
How you manage your waiting list as a&nbsp;
single waiting list across a geography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:22.960
which really gets to the heart of some of those&nbsp;
inequalities discussions that we've been having,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:22.960 --> 00:37:27.200
and that Ian just touched on a moment ago.&nbsp;
Those are, again, I think some of the ways that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:33.200
restoration and recovery will be best managed&nbsp;
as we go forward as a collective endeavour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:33.200 --> 00:37:36.640
rather than going back to the idea of,&nbsp;
and not that we've been there for some,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.640 --> 00:37:40.960
I should say, considerable time, the idea&nbsp;
that any part of the NHS stands alone.&nbsp;

00:37:42.720 --> 00:37:49.840
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Thank you very much,&nbsp;
Amanda. So Julian, the finance report, please.&nbsp;

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:57.360
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: So, on the published paper,&nbsp;
it shows that to the end of November,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:58.960 --> 00:38:03.440
we are spending over and above what otherwise&nbsp;
would have been, you know, the sort of original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:10.080
LTP mandate, £9.7 billion pounds. That is&nbsp;
basically consistent with the trend we've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:15.520
through this year as we've dealt with the impact&nbsp;
of COVID, the extra capacity we've had to buy, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:15.520 --> 00:38:21.360
financial arrangements actually for the discharge&nbsp;
scheme that was just being discussed earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:23.600 --> 00:38:30.480
as well as the loss of income to the NHS&nbsp;
that's happened as a result of COVID.&nbsp;

00:38:32.560 --> 00:38:38.640
In the detail underneath, expenditure is slightly&nbsp;
higher. That's because we've clearly done a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.640 --> 00:38:45.280
more activity as we have gone through&nbsp;
September, October, November. Whilst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:56.800
non-NHS income has recovered a bit over those&nbsp;
three months, capital, the report sets out where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.800 --> 00:39:03.200
we are, there is a normal hockey stick effect&nbsp;
in the NHS that we are still expecting to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:08.800
in terms of spend, notwithstanding, actually,&nbsp;
some of the issues, the NHS is grappling with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:08.800 --> 00:39:14.000
us as a result of COVID as people complete some of&nbsp;
the investment we are putting into mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:21.520
dorms, A&amp;E, critical infrastructure, some of our&nbsp;
oxygen supplies and numerous other things. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.640 --> 00:39:27.840
I don't think there's much more to be said at this&nbsp;
point, David. I'll take any questions or comments.&nbsp;

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:35.440
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I wonder if David Roberts&nbsp;
who chairs our finance group has got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:35.440 --> 00:39:40.480
would like to chip in at this point. David?
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: No, I think I think exactly as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:45.760
as Julian said, I think, you know,&nbsp;
alongside the sort of the theme of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:52.720
people and operational planning, which is clearly&nbsp;
the driver, we also need to be, and we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:52.720 --> 00:40:00.080
turning our mind to sort of financial planning.&nbsp;
Recognising I think the sensible decision which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:05.440
the board approved to sort of suspend operational&nbsp;
planning for the first quarter if you like in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:10.560
order to allow people to concentrate, so that a&nbsp;
financial framework comes in from sort of the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:11.280 --> 00:40:14.320
we start thinking about that,&nbsp;
again, in the new financial year.&nbsp;

00:40:15.120 --> 00:40:21.360
It will be important to join up operational&nbsp;
planning, quality, people and finances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.360 --> 00:40:26.480
and clearly those conversations will be&nbsp;
important with the treasury going forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:35.120
But so far, so good in terms of being able&nbsp;
to get the money into the places that it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:35.120 --> 00:40:39.120
needed. And I think the team have done&nbsp;
a good job on that. So that's it, David.&nbsp;

00:40:39.120 --> 00:40:44.880
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Thanks, David. Any&nbsp;
other questions for Julian on the finances?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:47.120 --> 00:40:51.680
Well, Julian, I'm sorry, I thought I&nbsp;
saw someone’s hand going up. Julian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.680 --> 00:40:56.720
thank you very much. I mean, you know, the&nbsp;
finance department is often the unsung hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.800 --> 00:41:05.360
at times like this, and I think keeping a grip&nbsp;
on the finances, whilst at the same time having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.360 --> 00:41:09.200
a probably greater degree of flexibility in&nbsp;
these times has been, you know, getting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:13.760
balance right is really difficult. And you've&nbsp;
just got it, I mean, all the feedback I get from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.320 --> 00:41:17.440
trusts is that they feel the relationship&nbsp;
with you, they can have an intelligent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.160 --> 00:41:22.160
sensible discussion with you, which is, I&nbsp;
think, you know, is very important. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:22.160 --> 00:41:27.520
taking them along with us on this journey.&nbsp;
So, thank you to you and all your team.&nbsp;

00:41:29.440 --> 00:41:34.560
So, I think we're now moving on&nbsp;
to the vaccine, an update on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:34.560 --> 00:41:39.200
vaccine project. Are you there, Emily?
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Yes, I'm here, David.&nbsp;

00:41:39.200 --> 00:41:46.280
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Over to you.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Thank you. So, there is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.120 --> 00:41:50.240
I will just talk through some of the&nbsp;
headlines from the paper and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:50.240 --> 00:41:56.800
obviously happy to have a discussion.
So, I mean, starting perhaps from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:56.800 --> 00:42:00.800
from something that Amanda said about&nbsp;
no part of the NHS standing alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.400 --> 00:42:05.840
I think the vaccination programme and its&nbsp;
deployment is a is a very good example of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:10.240
and indeed, of the whole, nobody in the country&nbsp;
standing alone. And of course, we wouldn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:12.240 --> 00:42:16.880
any vaccine distributed at all if it wasn't for&nbsp;
the work of the vaccines task force and obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:16.880 --> 00:42:20.880
all of the scientists and manufacturing involved&nbsp;
in getting the vaccines produced in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.880 --> 00:42:30.560
place, and of exceptional clinical leadership&nbsp;
in the MHRA’s approach to robust evaluation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:31.360 --> 00:42:35.840
of this critical intervention and the ACBI’s&nbsp;
advice to government, which has created the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:41.120
policy that the deployment team’s putting&nbsp;
into place. So, recognising that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:41.120 --> 00:42:45.280
there are multiple systems at work here.
And now in deployment, there are obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:49.040
some aspects which have been developed and are&nbsp;
being run nationally, like the supply chain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.000 --> 00:42:56.480
But nothing is going into arms without local&nbsp;
frontline clinical leaders, pharmacists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:57.760 --> 00:43:03.520
and the volunteers coming forward to&nbsp;
deliver this in various different locations.&nbsp;

00:43:03.520 --> 00:43:08.080
So just thinking about some of the ways that&nbsp;
deployment’s been enacted, we of course have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:08.720 --> 00:43:11.840
local vaccination services&nbsp;
which are led by primary care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:13.760 --> 00:43:19.600
Often groups of primary care networks working&nbsp;
together, but also now community pharmacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:20.240 --> 00:43:25.840
where there are over 100 sites operating to&nbsp;
deliver vaccine in the heart of local communities.&nbsp;

00:43:26.480 --> 00:43:32.000
We have hospital hubs who started off vaccinating&nbsp;
those most at risk and the over-80s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.280 --> 00:43:38.720
care home staff and have now moved on to working&nbsp;
as part of local health systems to deliver vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:38.720 --> 00:43:42.400
both to their own staff, but also to the rest&nbsp;
of health system staff and social care staff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:43.040 --> 00:43:48.480
And we have the vaccination centres, who are&nbsp;
working through, into all of those cohorts.&nbsp;

00:43:48.480 --> 00:43:52.400
And then critically, in order to reach the most&nbsp;
vulnerable, we of course have the roving models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:52.400 --> 00:44:00.320
both visits to care homes, and now also visits to&nbsp;
the housebound. And so, for example, yesterday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:00.320 --> 00:44:05.440
we had the story of a Holocaust survivor on&nbsp;
Holocaust Memorial Day being vaccinated in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:05.440 --> 00:44:14.480
her own home by her GP. And today, we have an&nbsp;
alliance in the Crawley health system, between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:14.480 --> 00:44:21.200
Metrobus and the Gurjar Hindu temple locally,&nbsp;
and the Appletree centre to have a vaccine bus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:21.200 --> 00:44:27.760
to reach the communities that need the vaccine.
So really, the NHS and associated volunteers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:27.760 --> 00:44:33.280
supporters going all out to get the vaccine to&nbsp;
those who are, who are most in need. Clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.280 --> 00:44:37.680
recognising Ian's comment, you know, that's never&nbsp;
going to be enough. We have to constantly keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.680 --> 00:44:42.640
looking at who we're reaching and making sure&nbsp;
that we work closely with local government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:42.640 --> 00:44:46.880
as Ian mentioned, to get to communities&nbsp;
who may be more reluctant to come forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:52.320
but doing everything we can to make access not&nbsp;
the issue and working with communities directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:52.320 --> 00:44:55.760
and particularly local governments, as I&nbsp;
said, to make sure people get the vaccine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:55.760 --> 00:44:58.080
people to come forward for the&nbsp;
vaccine when it's offered to them.&nbsp;

00:44:59.600 --> 00:45:05.760
So, at the moment, we have sufficient capacity to&nbsp;
deliver the vaccine that arrives in the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:05.760 --> 00:45:11.920
each week. And multiple teams, as I've mentioned,&nbsp;
getting together to make sure that happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:17.120
using both existing IT systems and the newly&nbsp;
developed national booking system to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:17.120 --> 00:45:23.120
people get timely invitations and can come in and&nbsp;
receive the vaccination in a planned way, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:23.120 --> 00:45:29.360
sequence directed by the policy advice by JCVI to&nbsp;
the government and getting to the most vulnerable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:29.360 --> 00:45:34.080
first. And the statistics being published&nbsp;
externally every day shows that we're making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:35.280 --> 00:45:39.040
really good progress towards reaching&nbsp;
all of those in cohorts one to four with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:39.040 --> 00:45:43.520
an offer of vaccination by the target set&nbsp;
by the minister on the 15th of February.&nbsp;

00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:50.560
So, with a particular call out to the local&nbsp;
directors of public health, who are working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.560 --> 00:45:56.640
very closely through the local government link&nbsp;
to get to their local communities, making sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:56.640 --> 00:46:00.320
so that's sort of where we're at. Obviously,&nbsp;
beyond the 15th of February, we need to continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:00.320 --> 00:46:07.600
vaccinating the cohorts laid out by the JCVI,&nbsp;
so all those over 50, including those who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:07.600 --> 00:46:16.000
vulnerable under 50, who are in cohort six. And&nbsp;
we also need to plan ahead to make sure we think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:16.000 --> 00:46:21.520
beyond, you know, the capacity that we need right&nbsp;
now into the capacity to reach those beyond the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:21.520 --> 00:46:26.080
current deployment plan set by the government&nbsp;
and make sure we've got a plan for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:26.080 --> 00:46:31.760
and for also next winter, working closely with the&nbsp;
flu programme to make sure we make the most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:37.440
vaccine infrastructure that's been established.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Emily, well you’ve done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:37.440 --> 00:46:42.480
it's been a remarkable achievement, what you've&nbsp;
done over the last, well, six, I mean, well two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:48.240
months anyway, it's been absolutely phenomenal.&nbsp;
So many congratulations to you and your team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:48.800 --> 00:46:53.440
I wonder if I could just bring in&nbsp;
Wol. You’ve raised issues before about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:53.440 --> 00:46:58.560
access to people from BAME communities and&nbsp;
minority ethnic communities around the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:58.560 --> 00:47:02.560
Do you just want to explore that a little bit?
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Yeah, I mean, just to comment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:02.560 --> 00:47:07.840
This is with my other hat on. I chair&nbsp;
the Guy’s and St Thomas's charity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:13.600
which is based in Lambeth and Southwark&nbsp;
and has the particular advantage of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:13.600 --> 00:47:19.360
routed to the local system. And so, we get to&nbsp;
try out lots of things in a very granular level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:19.360 --> 00:47:23.840
some of which can then be exported out&nbsp;
and copied across the rest of the system.&nbsp;

00:47:24.480 --> 00:47:29.600
And we've been doing quite a lot of work on&nbsp;
vaccine hesitancy and trying to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:29.600 --> 00:47:34.400
at the very, very, sort of street-by-street&nbsp;
level, what is it within some BAME communities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:34.400 --> 00:47:40.160
that means they will not necessarily take on&nbsp;
board the message that is being delivered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:40.160 --> 00:47:46.560
And it's a very nuanced debate. And it's actually&nbsp;
sort of quite involved. But things like trust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:47.120 --> 00:47:51.600
are a big issue, and not just of national&nbsp;
issues, but of local systems, which is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:51.600 --> 00:47:57.200
bit disappointing. And again, they're bringing&nbsp;
up issues around who is best placed to deliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:57.200 --> 00:48:02.640
the message about why it's safe. And out of&nbsp;
caution, some of this from this experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:04.160 --> 00:48:09.680
if I call them celebrity endorsers work up to&nbsp;
a point, but not necessarily the full point.&nbsp;

00:48:10.960 --> 00:48:16.960
And then of course, it's the message around the&nbsp;
young, and them feeling, actually, I'm okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:16.960 --> 00:48:23.760
this is not worth the risk. And then other sort of&nbsp;
issues like speed of delivery, etc, etc, speed of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:23.760 --> 00:48:28.640
creation of vaccine, etc. So, we've got a quite&nbsp;
a challenge here. And I know Emily you're totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:28.640 --> 00:48:33.520
focused on this area, as well as many others.&nbsp;
But we're going to have to think very hard about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:33.520 --> 00:48:39.520
the way we respond and really lean into those,&nbsp;
there’s perhaps in some cases very valid issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:39.520 --> 00:48:45.520
because let's be honest, the health system has&nbsp;
not necessarily been a friend to many communities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:45.520 --> 00:48:50.880
And we just need to make sure we listen, hear&nbsp;
those concerns and make sure we sort of can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:50.880 --> 00:48:54.960
them to a place where they want to get vaccinated&nbsp;
and are vaccinated otherwise some of the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:54.960 --> 00:49:00.320
we're doing will be for naught.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:00.320 --> 00:49:02.320
Shall I comment on that, David?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Please, yeah.&nbsp;

00:49:02.320 --> 00:49:05.920
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: The first thing I want&nbsp;
to say is I'm really grateful for your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:05.920 --> 00:49:10.800
sort of thanks and praise, and I know that other&nbsp;
board members have said at other times. I do want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:10.800 --> 00:49:16.800
to keep emphasising, you know, this is a massive&nbsp;
team effort, right. And I really appreciate that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:16.800 --> 00:49:20.720
thanks, but I want to make sure that things are&nbsp;
going to this extraordinary network of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:20.720 --> 00:49:24.640
who are making this happen, some of whom are very&nbsp;
visible, and some of whom are much less visible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:24.640 --> 00:49:30.080
So, recognising I think that the strength of the&nbsp;
marshalling call of obviously this public health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:30.080 --> 00:49:34.880
crisis and also the way the NHS is able to, to&nbsp;
mobilise to deliver something this challenging.&nbsp;

00:49:34.880 --> 00:49:37.520
So, I'm not saying no, thank&nbsp;
you. I'm just saying I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:37.520 --> 00:49:41.280
need that praise and recognition spread around all&nbsp;
of the people who are involved because it’s a lot.&nbsp;

00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:48.720
So just on Wol’s point, as you say, absolute&nbsp;
critical issue. You know, there is a health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:48.720 --> 00:49:52.800
inequalities team, a qualities team actually,&nbsp;
embedded in the programme with a committee that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:54.560 --> 00:49:59.440
is very cross-agency involving the&nbsp;
directors of public health, obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:00.560 --> 00:50:04.160
the Cabinet Office, there are other&nbsp;
government equalities groups, local leaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:04.800 --> 00:50:10.640
and faith leaders and the government, the NHS&nbsp;
observatory on race equality, for example. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:10.640 --> 00:50:14.720
really trying to draw those stakeholders together.
I think there are two parts to this, aren’t there?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:19.760
One is the offer. And in the deployment programme,&nbsp;
we are absolutely making sure that the offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:19.760 --> 00:50:23.200
is universal, and that there is nothing&nbsp;
that stands in the way of somebody being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:23.200 --> 00:50:28.480
offered a vaccine, including over the last&nbsp;
few weeks making sure we have a resolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:28.480 --> 00:50:31.920
to the challenge of people who don't have an&nbsp;
NHS number and how they access the vaccine.&nbsp;

00:50:34.560 --> 00:50:38.320
And then the other point is the one that&nbsp;
you raised, which is about acceptance and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:43.600
and coming forward when you are offered. And&nbsp;
that's where, you know, we're working, as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:43.600 --> 00:50:49.760
described super closely with the local authorities&nbsp;
and with local directors of public health, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:49.760 --> 00:50:54.960
already do this work every day in other vaccine&nbsp;
programmes and other public health issues because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:54.960 --> 00:50:59.680
they have knowledge resources, local connections,&nbsp;
relationships, which it's not right for the NHS to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:59.680 --> 00:51:04.480
