&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Laura. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:21.120 --> 00:53:26.640
I'd like to bring in Munir, Munir, because&nbsp;
the one of the more controversial aspects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:26.640 --> 00:53:32.720
the programme has been the 12-week delay of this&nbsp;
for the second, the second, the second vaccine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:32.720 --> 00:53:36.000
or the second dose. You couldn't just give&nbsp;
your perspective on that, because you were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:36.000 --> 00:53:42.480
very much part of that decision, I think.
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed: So, we have had a look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:47.600
the data that was provided by the companies&nbsp;
through the trials, and the predominant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:48.320 --> 00:53:54.560
impact of the vaccine of the first dose,&nbsp;
it gives you a large amount of immunity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:54.560 --> 00:54:01.280
The second dose adds a bit of more immunity, but&nbsp;
which provides you with a longer-term protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:01.280 --> 00:54:06.880
from the infection. So, it's felt that by&nbsp;
extending the interval between the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:06.880 --> 00:54:12.960
and second dose to 12 weeks would provide the&nbsp;
immunity that's needed to prevent infections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:12.960 --> 00:54:19.440
but also would allow more people to be able to&nbsp;
get the vaccine given the supply issues. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:19.440 --> 00:54:25.680
I think the overall impact of this will be that we&nbsp;
will save more lives and protect many more people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:26.240 --> 00:54:30.160
through this kind of process.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Munir. Simon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.160 --> 00:54:34.160
you wanted to come in on this, I think.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:34.160 --> 00:54:38.720
Thank you, David. It was actually just on&nbsp;
the data question and cracking uptake in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:38.720 --> 00:54:45.520
response to Wol’s very powerful intervention.&nbsp;
First thing to say is that national data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:46.400 --> 00:54:53.520
are now being published weekly on the breakdown&nbsp;
of uptake including by race and ethnicity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:53.520 --> 00:55:00.160
This afternoon will be the first of the weekly&nbsp;
national publications. But more importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:00.160 --> 00:55:06.240
at a much more local level, those data split&nbsp;
into 16 different categories are available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:06.240 --> 00:55:14.160
to local authorities and to directors of public&nbsp;
health, and data at a much more granular level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:14.160 --> 00:55:20.160
of about sort of 7,000 population, so sort of&nbsp;
close to ward level, is also now regularly flowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:20.160 --> 00:55:23.920
twice weekly to directors of public health, and&nbsp;
they've obviously got a crucial role in helping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:24.640 --> 00:55:28.960
respond to some of these inequalities.
The job of the vaccines programme, Emily's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:28.960 --> 00:55:35.200
programme, is to make sure that the equal and&nbsp;
accessible offer is made to everybody. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:35.200 --> 00:55:40.480
we've got to work with partners, locally strong&nbsp;
role for faith groups, for community groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:40.480 --> 00:55:45.120
for local government, to get that uptake.
But I think just one final word of caution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:46.160 --> 00:55:53.440
It is not, I don't think, very sensible&nbsp;
to talk about so called BAME uptake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:53.440 --> 00:56:00.480
as if it were a single explanatory category,&nbsp;
because quite frankly, when you actually look more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:00.480 --> 00:56:05.760
carefully, you see that there are different&nbsp;
degrees of access barriers or vaccine hesitancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:06.480 --> 00:56:12.640
or trust in public services amongst different&nbsp;
communities. And in fact, even amongst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:12.640 --> 00:56:20.320
people described as from South Asian heritage,&nbsp;
uptake is much higher amongst people of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:20.320 --> 00:56:26.720
Indian heritage than of Pakistani or Bangladeshi&nbsp;
heritage, for example. So that together with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:27.280 --> 00:56:32.640
black British, black Caribbean, and black&nbsp;
African groups, as described using the ONS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:32.640 --> 00:56:37.280
classification, that's the level of granularity,&nbsp;
the local engagement that Wol talked about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:37.280 --> 00:56:45.040
that actually will make the difference here even&nbsp;
as the programme rolls out to many, millions more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:45.040 --> 00:56:49.520
in different age groups and categories of risk.
Wol Kolade: David, if I might just come in. Simon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:49.520 --> 00:56:53.840
that's a very, very important dimension, actually.&nbsp;
And actually, I find myself, I'm just going to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:53.840 --> 00:56:59.680
